"""Phase relocation and detection inside the stable Steinberg group."""

def append_detection(p, eq, h, sub, page):
    page('20. Long entries and opposite roots')
    p('Work over the particular algebra <i>R</i>(<i>p</i>) of Theorem 18.1. Fix one phase modulus ℓ supplied by Section 19, its threshold <i>N</i>, and an integer <i>M</i> ≥ 2<i>N</i> + <i>k</i> + 1. Every group in Sections 20-22 is stable. A nonzero word is also used to denote its basis class. Its length and its long-word phases are well defined on that class.')
    p('<b>Lemma 20.1 (Moving a cut).</b> <i>If ca = 0, then for three distinct indices i,h,j and arbitrary b,</i>')
    eq('[x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>ab</i>), x<sub>ji</sub>(<i>c</i>)] = [x<sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>), x<sub>hi</sub>(<i>bc</i>)].')
    p('<i>In particular [x<sub>ij</sub>(a),x<sub>ji</sub>(c)] is independent of j. It centralizes both factors, has order dividing p, and has matrix image 1 + acE<sub>ii</sub>.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Apply the three-factor identity of Section 10 to <i>X</i> = x<sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>), <i>Y</i> = x<sub>hj</sub>(<i>b</i>), <i>Z</i> = x<sub>ji</sub>(<i>c</i>). Their consecutive commutators are <i>A</i> = x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>ab</i>) and <i>B</i> = x<sub>hi</sub>(<i>bc</i>). The required commuting pairs follow from their positions, or from <i>ca</i> = 0 and (<i>bc</i>)(<i>ab</i>) = 0. The identity follows. Setting <i>b</i> = 1 changes the auxiliary index arbitrarily. Use an index outside {<i>i,j</i>} to express this commutator; each of its two factors commutes with both original factors, since the only possible consecutive parameter is <i>ca</i>. If <i>C</i> is the commutator, [x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>a</i>)<sup>p</sup>,x<sub>ji</sub>(<i>c</i>)] = <i>C</i><sup>p</sup> = 1. Finally multiply the four elementary matrices; <i>ca</i> = 0 cancels all extra entries except <i>acE</i><sub>ii</sub>. □')
    p('<b>Lemma 20.2.</b> <i>For a nonzero class w with |w| ≥ M and σ(w) ≠ τ(w), there is an element H<sub>i</sub>(w), independent of any auxiliary coordinate, such that whenever u,v are nonzero words with |u|,|v| ≥ N, |u|+|v| ≥ M and σ(u) ≠ τ(v),</i>')
    eq('[x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>u</i>),x<sub>ji</sub>(<i>v</i>)] = <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>uv</i>) &nbsp; if <i>uv</i> ≠ 0,<br/>[x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>u</i>),x<sub>ji</sub>(<i>v</i>)] = 1 &nbsp; if <i>uv</i> = 0.')
    p('<i>The element H<sub>i</sub>(w) has order dividing p and matrix image 1+wE<sub>ii</sub>.</i> The zero alternative is a group assertion; vanishing matrix products alone would not justify commutation of opposite roots.')

    page('20. Independence of the word and its cut')
    p('<b>Proof of Lemma 20.2.</b> The mismatch implies <i>vu</i> = 0 by Section 19. To move a cut right, write the word as <i>abc</i> with <i>a</i> = <i>u</i>, <i>bc</i> = <i>v</i> and |<i>c</i>| ≥ <i>N</i>. Its end phase is τ(<i>c</i>) = τ(<i>v</i>), so <i>ca</i> = 0. Lemma 20.1 identifies the commutators for <i>a</i> | <i>bc</i> and <i>ab</i> | <i>c</i>. A move left uses the same formula with <i>ab</i> = <i>u</i> and <i>c</i> = <i>v</i>. The outer phases stay unchanged when the new parts are nonzero. If either new part is zero, the equality already proves the original commutator trivial.')
    p('For a word of length <i>n</i> ≥ <i>M</i>, legal cut positions range from <i>N</i> to <i>n</i> − <i>N</i>. A relation of length at most <i>k</i> straddles at most <i>k</i> − 1 of them. Since <i>M</i> ≥ 2<i>N</i> + <i>k</i> + 1, choose a legal cut for which that relation lies wholly in one part, moving the cut as above. Replacing within that part leaves its ring element and, if nonzero, its phase unchanged. Thus the commutator is preserved under any replacement, unless it has already been proved trivial by a zero part.')
    p('If the concatenation is nonzero, every equivalent word and every one of its factors is nonzero. Cut movement and replacement therefore prove dependence only on the concatenation class. For any class <i>w</i> in the statement, choose a cut with both lengths at least <i>N</i>. Its parts inherit σ(<i>w</i>) and τ(<i>w</i>), so the mismatch holds; define <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>) by that commutator.')
    p('If the concatenation is zero, Lemma 16.1 supplies a replacement path to a word containing a declared zero factor. Follow the path with movable cuts. If neither part became zero earlier, choose the final cut with the zero factor entirely in one part. That part is zero in the algebra, so its root is the identity and the commutator is one. The order and matrix image statements follow from Lemma 20.1. □')
    sub('A subgroup on which the matrix map is faithful')
    p('Let <i>S</i> be a finite coordinate set with assigned phases <i>P</i>(<i>i</i>) modulo ℓ. A long entry <i>w</i> from <i>i</i> to <i>j</i> is consistent if σ(<i>w</i>) = <i>P</i>(<i>i</i>) and τ(<i>w</i>) = <i>P</i>(<i>j</i>). Call it a wrong-row entry if instead')
    eq('|<i>w</i>| ≥ <i>M</i>, &nbsp; σ(<i>w</i>) ≠ <i>P</i>(<i>i</i>), &nbsp; τ(<i>w</i>) = <i>P</i>(<i>j</i>).')
    p('Use x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>) for a wrong-row entry off the diagonal and <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>) on the diagonal. Let <i>U</i>(<i>S,P</i>) be the subgroup they generate.')

    page('20. Faithfulness and normalization of the extra factors')
    p('<b>Lemma 20.3.</b> <i>The group U(S,P) is abelian of exponent dividing p, its matrix map is injective, and every consistent root normalizes it.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> For an off-diagonal wrong-row entry x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>a</i>) and another off-diagonal root x<sub>ef</sub>(<i>b</i>) with wrong-row or consistent entry, a forward consecutive commutator has parameter <i>ab</i>. It is zero for a wrong-row <i>b</i>, since τ(<i>a</i>) = <i>P</i>(<i>j</i>) ≠ σ(<i>b</i>). For consistent <i>b</i>, a nonzero <i>ab</i> inherits the outside phases and is again wrong-row. A reverse consecutive product <i>ba</i> is always zero since τ(<i>b</i>) = <i>P</i>(<i>i</i>) ≠ σ(<i>a</i>).')
    p('For opposite positions the same last mismatch permits Lemma 20.2. The commutator is <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>ab</i>) if <i>ab</i> is nonzero and is one otherwise; in the former case it is a diagonal wrong-row entry. If both roots are wrong-row, both products are zero. All remaining pairs commute by their positions. This treats all off-diagonal generators.')
    p('Represent a diagonal <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>) as a commutator through a coordinate outside <i>S</i>. A root on <i>S</i> disjoint from <i>i</i> commutes with it. For a root involving <i>i</i>, the faithful strip between this two-coordinate support and its complement gives the exact conjugation rules')
    eq('<i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>) x<sub>if</sub>(<i>b</i>) <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>)<sup>−1</sup> = x<sub>if</sub>(<i>b</i>+<i>wb</i>),')
    eq('<i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>) x<sub>ei</sub>(<i>b</i>) <i>H</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>w</i>)<sup>−1</sup> = x<sub>ei</sub>(<i>b</i>−<i>bw</i>).')
    p('Indeed <i>w</i><sup>2</sup> = 0 by its phase mismatch, so the inverse matrix has diagonal correction −<i>w</i>. The incoming product <i>bw</i> is zero by phases. The outgoing product <i>wb</i> is zero for wrong-row <i>b</i> and, when nonzero for consistent <i>b</i>, is wrong-row. Hence these formulas prove the required commutations and normalization for diagonal generators. The minus sign is retained for odd characteristic.')
    p('Diagonal generators at different coordinates can be represented on disjoint pairs of coordinates. At the same coordinate, write a second generator as [x<sub>it</sub>(<i>a</i>),x<sub>ti</sub>(<i>c</i>)] with <i>ac</i> = <i>w</i>′ and both parts of length at least <i>N</i>. Use a new auxiliary <i>t</i>. Their phases give <i>wa</i> = <i>cw</i> = 0, so the first generator fixes both factors by the same strip action. Thus all generators of <i>U</i> commute and have order dividing <i>p</i>.')
    p('Every pair of correction matrices multiplies to zero: a coordinate match forces the end phase of the first word to differ from the start phase of the second. A collected group element therefore has matrix 1 + ∑ <i>c</i><sub>ijw</sub><i>wE</i><sub>ij</sub>, with coefficients in F<sub>p</sub>. The nonzero word classes form a basis, so an identity matrix forces all coefficients zero. The collected element is then one. Finally the commutator computations give conjugation into <i>U</i> by consistent roots; their inverses are their (<i>p</i>−1)st powers, so they normalize <i>U</i>. □')

    page('21. Relocating a central element according to phases')
    p('<b>Lemma 21.1.</b> <i>Suppose z ∈ J(R) has a root-word representative ∏<sub>ν</sub>x<sub>iν,jν</sub>(w<sub>ν</sub>) in nonzero word classes of lengths at least M. In coordinates (i,s), with s ∈ ℤ/ℓℤ,</i>')
    eq('<i>z</i> = ∏<sub>ν</sub> x<sub>(iν,σ(wν)),(jν,τ(wν))</sub>(<i>w</i><sub>ν</sub>).')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Let <i>A</i> = F<sub>p</sub> ⊕ <i>R</i><sub>≥M</sub>. Choose numerical columns <i>u</i><sub>s</sub> and rows <i>v</i><sub>s</sub> of size ℓ with <i>v</i><sub>s</sub><i>u</i><sub>s</sub> = 1. On its long-word basis set')
    eq('ρ(<i>w</i>) = <i>u</i><sub>σ(w)</sub><i>v</i><sub>τ(w)</sub><i>w</i>, &nbsp; ρ(1) = 1<sub>ℓ</sub>.')
    p('This extends to a unital homomorphism <i>A</i> → M<sub>ℓ</sub>(<i>R</i>). A nonzero product has matching middle phases, so its middle numerical pairing is one and its outside phases are correct. A zero product contributes the zero entry regardless of the numerical pairing. Linear extension and scalar multiplication complete the check. The original root word has identity matrix already over <i>A</i>, since <i>A</i> embeds in <i>R</i>. Hence every such evaluation followed by block roots belongs to <i>J</i>(<i>R</i>). No injectivity of St(<i>A</i>) → St(<i>R</i>) is needed.')
    p('Begin with all <i>u</i><sub>s</sub> = e<sub>0</sub> and all <i>v</i><sub>s</sub> = e<sub>0</sub>*. The evaluated word is the coordinate copy of <i>z</i> in inner index zero and hence equals <i>z</i> by centrality and Lemma 11.3. First change the rows to <i>v</i><sub>0</sub> = e<sub>0</sub>* and <i>v</i><sub>t</sub> = e<sub>0</sub>* + e<sub>t</sub>* for <i>t</i> ≠ 0. The added factors run from occupied inner-zero coordinates to unused coordinates. They lie in a faithful strip normalized by the original roots. Collect them on the right. Both old and new evaluations have identity matrix, so the strip correction is one. The evaluation is still <i>z</i>.')
    p('Let <i>Q</i> have these new rows. It is invertible, <i>v</i><sub>t</sub><i>Q</i><sup>−1</sup> = e<sub>t</sub>*, and <i>Q</i>e<sub>0</sub> = ∑<sub>r</sub>e<sub>r</sub>. Apply the exact numerical conjugation of Section 14 to replace the columns by this sum and the rows by e<sub>t</sub>*. Centrality leaves the evaluation unchanged. Each block root is now ∏<sub>r</sub>x<sub>(i,r),(j,τ(w))</sub>(<i>w</i>). Give coordinate (<i>i,s</i>) phase <i>s</i>. Keep its consistent factor with <i>r</i> = σ(<i>w</i>) and collect all other factors into <i>U</i> by Lemma 20.3. The retained word has identity matrix too, by the homomorphism ρ with columns e<sub>s</sub> and rows e<sub>s</sub>*. The collected <i>U</i>-element has identity matrix and is one. This proves the formula. □')

    page('21. Cyclic evaluation is a power in the same group')
    p('<b>Proposition 21.2.</b> <i>For z with a representative as in Lemma 21.1, its graded cyclic evaluation satisfies W<sub>z</sub>(C<sub>ℓ</sub>) = z<sup>ℓ</sup> in St(R).</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> A homogeneous word <i>w</i> has degree |<i>w</i>|. Its graded evaluation at <i>C</i><sub>ℓ</sub> gives a block root with entry <i>wC</i><sub>ℓ</sub><sup>|w|</sup>. Since τ(<i>w</i>) − σ(<i>w</i>) = |<i>w</i>| modulo ℓ, its constituent factors can be written')
    eq('x<sub>(i,σ(w)+a),(j,τ(w)+a)</sub>(<i>w</i>), &nbsp; <i>a</i> ∈ ℤ/ℓℤ.')
    p('Factors with different offsets commute, including factors coming from different original roots. If the column of one matches the row of another, τ(<i>w</i>) + <i>a</i> = σ(<i>w</i>′) + <i>a</i>′. For different offsets this means τ(<i>w</i>) ≠ σ(<i>w</i>′), so <i>ww</i>′ = 0. A reverse match similarly implies <i>w</i>′<i>w</i> = 0. The defining root relations prove commutation unless the positions are opposite. In that case the reverse match also gives σ(<i>w</i>) ≠ τ(<i>w</i>′); Lemma 20.2, with its zero-product alternative, proves the needed group commutation.')
    p('Collect by offset, retaining the original order inside each offset. Offset zero is the phase-relocated representative and hence equals <i>z</i> by Lemma 21.1. Every other offset is its coordinate permutation and also equals <i>z</i> by Lemma 11.3. There are ℓ offsets, proving <i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>ℓ</sub>) = <i>z</i><sup>ℓ</sup>. □')
    p('All statements so far were for roots whose parameters are basis word classes. General coefficients in F<sub>p</sub> cause no restriction: write a scalar coefficient as an integer between 0 and <i>p</i> − 1 and use root addition to replace it by that many occurrences of the root. Inverse roots use <i>p</i> − 1 copies. This preserves the degree lower bound and gives a finite word, so the arguments apply in every prime characteristic.')

    page('22. Long representatives and stable separation')
    p('<b>Lemma 22.1.</b> <i>Every element killed by St(R) → St(R/R<sub>≥2M</sub>) admits a finite representative in roots of nonzero word classes of lengths at least M.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> For any ideal <i>K</i> the kernel of St(<i>R</i>) → St(<i>R</i>/<i>K</i>) is the normal closure of its roots with parameters in <i>K</i>. Killing these roots makes parameters depend only on their residue class by addition, and the remaining presentation is exactly that of the quotient ring. For <i>K</i> = <i>R</i><sub>≥2M</sub>, expand every parameter in the word basis and its coefficients as repeated roots. It suffices to consider <i>g</i>x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>)<i>g</i><sup>−1</sup> with |<i>w</i>| ≥ 2<i>M</i>.')
    p('Split a representative <i>w</i> = <i>ab</i> with both parts of length at least <i>M</i>. They are nonzero because <i>w</i> is nonzero. Let <i>S</i> contain <i>i,j</i> and the finite support of a fixed root word for <i>g</i>, and choose <i>h</i> outside <i>S</i>. Then')
    eq('<i>g</i>x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>)<i>g</i><sup>−1</sup><br/>= [<i>g</i>x<sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>)<i>g</i><sup>−1</sup>, <i>g</i>x<sub>hj</sub>(<i>b</i>)<i>g</i><sup>−1</sup>].')
    p('Writing <i>A</i><sub>g</sub> for its matrix on <i>S</i>, exact strip conjugation gives respective root parameters (<i>A</i><sub>g</sub>)<sub>ri</sub><i>a</i> and <i>b</i>(<i>A</i><sub>g</sub><sup>−1</sup>)<sub>js</sub>. They all belong to the ideal <i>R</i><sub>≥M</sub>. Expand the strips, their entries and the commutator as a finite root word; inverse coefficients can again be expanded into repeated roots. Every entry has length at least <i>M</i>. Apply this to the finitely many conjugates in a normal-closure expression. □')
    p('<b>Theorem 22.2 (Stable separation).</b> <i>For every prime p, the constructed algebra R(p) satisfies</i>')
    eq('⋂<sub>d≥1</sub> ker[St(<i>R</i>(<i>p</i>)) → St(<i>R</i>(<i>p</i>)/<i>R</i>(<i>p</i>)<sub>≥d</sub>)] = {1}.')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Let <i>z</i> belong to the intersection. Its finite matrix image is the identity modulo every degree ideal, whose intersection is zero. Thus <i>z</i> ∈ <i>J</i>(<i>R</i>); truncation at degree one gives trivial reduction in St(F<sub>p</sub>). Theorem 14.1 gives <i>z</i><sup>pᵃ</sup> = 1. Fix one root word for <i>z</i>. The wrap-removal argument of Section 14 supplies a bound beyond which <i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>b</sub>) = 1 for every integer <i>b</i>.')
    p('Choose a phase modulus ℓ coprime to <i>p</i> above that bound, using Section 19. Only now choose its phase threshold <i>N</i> and <i>M</i> ≥ 2<i>N</i> + <i>k</i> + 1. Since <i>z</i> is killed at degree 2<i>M</i>, Lemma 22.1 gives a long-entry representative. Proposition 21.2 proves <i>z</i><sup>ℓ</sup> = <i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>ℓ</sub>) = 1. The operation <i>W</i><sub>z</sub> depends on the group element, not its representative, so the earlier bound remains valid. Coprimality and Bézout’s identity yield <i>z</i> = 1. □')
